  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 AM EST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nA trough working its way through the mid-Missippi valley today \nbrings plenty of clouds to our local area while the best rain chance \nassociated with this features resides outside of our forecast area \nto the south. I did carry 10% POPs for the day in preparation for \nany rogue sprinkles in this period of continued, weak WAA and ascent.\n\nRain chances increase in the days ahead as the 500-mb pattern \nbecomes highly amplified due to a deep trough emerging from the \nRockies. Gulf moisture seeps in atop a mid-Atlantic ridge, perhaps \nleading to isoalted showers Monday afternoon. Low confidence on this \nas the best moisture pooling is off to the west, yet available CAMs \ndo litter portions of the area with isolated showers. Very warm and \nbreezy Tuesday as one low occludes over the UP and cyclogenesis \nrenews over the Central Plains. We'll see high temperatures soar \nwell into the 70s (challenging records) depending on cloud cover. \nSimilarly, the magnitude of any synoptic wind gusts will hinge on \nbreaks in the clouds aiding to mix down a strengthening low-level \njet. Poor lapse rates and a lack of instability look to keep the \nsevere weather risk in check Wednesday as the occluded front moves \nthrough in the first half of the day. With the low lingering over \nthe Midwest Thursday, breezy conditions may persist while rain \nchances pause within the dry slot of this expansive system. \n\nNot a ton of cold air behind this low with ridging noted in the Lee \nof the Rockies. The result is high temperatures to finish out the \nweek right near 60 with lows near 40. Low shower chances may persist \nthrough the end of the week until the low finally exits over the \nNortheast.",
